## Solution

For this challenge, I followed the mark-bit
flipping technique from the uploaded
suggested solution.

The main idea is to change what the
Boolean marking value means after each
garbage collection cycle.

Instead of having true always mean
"marked" and false always mean
"unmarked," the VM maintains a Boolean
that tells us which value currently
represents a marked object.

After each garbage collection cycle,
the VM flips that Boolean value.

This means surviving objects automatically
become logically unmarked for the next
collection without having to update
their individual marking fields.

## 1. Understanding the Original Implementation

In Chapter 26, clox uses a mark-and-sweep
garbage collector.

During the marking phase, the collector
starts with the VM's roots and follows
references to identify all reachable objects.

Reachable objects are marked by setting:

```c
object->isMarked = true;
```

During sweeping, the collector checks
whether each allocated object is marked.

The original code includes:

```c
if (object->isMarked) {
  object->isMarked = false;
  previous = object;
  object = object->next;
} else {
  // Remove and free unreachable objects.
}
```

If an object is marked, the collector
keeps it in memory.

It also resets isMarked to false so
the object can be marked again during
the next garbage collection cycle.

Although this works correctly, it
requires a write to every surviving
object during each sweep.

I wanted to avoid those repeated writes.

## 2. Add a Marking State to the VM

The suggested solution introduces a
new Boolean field in the VM structure.

In vm.h, add:

```c
bool markValue;
```

This field defines which Boolean
value currently means "marked."

For example:

```text
vm.markValue = true
```

Means:

```text
Object mark = true   -> Marked
Object mark = false  -> Unmarked
```

After the VM flips its markValue:

```text
vm.markValue = false
```

The meanings reverse:

```text
Object mark = false  -> Marked
Object mark = true   -> Unmarked
```

The important point is that an
object's marking state is determined
by comparing its stored bit with
the current VM marking value.

We no longer assume that true
always represents a marked object.

## 3. Initialize the VM Marking Value

In vm.c, initialize the new field
inside initVM():

```c
vm.markValue = true;
```

This should happen before any
allocation can trigger garbage
collection or use the marking state.

The initial value can be either
true or false.

I chose true because it matches
the original implementation's
meaning during the first collection.

## 4. Rename the Object Marking Field

In object.h, the original Obj
structure contains:

```c
struct sObj {
  ObjType type;
  bool isMarked;
  struct Obj* next;
};
```

I would rename isMarked to mark:

```c
struct sObj {
  ObjType type;
  bool mark;
  struct Obj* next;
};
```

This makes the new behavior clearer.

The field is no longer a Boolean
that independently tells us whether
the object is marked.

Instead, its value must be compared
with vm.markValue to determine the
object's current marking state.

## 5. Update Object Allocation

In the original implementation,
new objects start unmarked:

```c
object->isMarked = false;
```

With mark-bit flipping, false
does not always mean unmarked.

A new object must instead have a
mark value opposite to the current
VM marking value.

In object.c, update allocateObject():

```c
static Obj* allocateObject(size_t size,
                           ObjType type) {
  Obj* object = (Obj*)reallocate(NULL, 0, size);

  object->type = type;

  // New objects begin logically unmarked.
  object->mark = !vm.markValue;

  object->next = vm.objects;
  vm.objects = object;

#ifdef DEBUG_LOG_GC
  printf("%p allocate %zu for %d\n",
         (void*)object, size, type);
#endif

  return object;
}
```

The important change is:

```c
object->mark = !vm.markValue;
```

Regardless of whether the VM currently
uses true or false to represent marked
objects, newly allocated objects
start in the unmarked state.

## 6. Update markObject()

The markObject() function determines
whether a reachable object has already
been marked.

Originally, it checks:

```c
if (object->isMarked) return;
```

With the new approach, I would change
this comparison to:

```c
if (object->mark == vm.markValue) return;
```

When marking an object, replace:

```c
object->isMarked = true;
```

With:

```c
object->mark = vm.markValue;
```

The relevant updated function in
memory.c is:

```c
void markObject(Obj* object) {
  if (object == NULL) return;

  // Already marked in this collection.
  if (object->mark == vm.markValue) return;

#ifdef DEBUG_LOG_GC
  printf("%p mark ", (void*)object);
  printValue(OBJ_VAL(object));
  printf("\n");
#endif

  // Mark the object using the current VM value.
  object->mark = vm.markValue;

  if (vm.grayCapacity < vm.grayCount + 1) {
    vm.grayCapacity =
        GROW_CAPACITY(vm.grayCapacity);

    vm.grayStack = (Obj**)realloc(
        vm.grayStack,
        sizeof(Obj*) * vm.grayCapacity);

    if (vm.grayStack == NULL) exit(1);
  }

  vm.grayStack[vm.grayCount++] = object;
}
```

This preserves the original behavior
of preventing an object from being
processed more than once per collection.

The only difference is how we determine
whether the object is marked.

## 7. Update the Weak String Table

Chapter 26 explains that clox stores
interned strings in a table.

This table uses weak references because
its entries should not prevent unreachable
strings from being collected.

Before sweeping, the VM removes entries
for strings that are no longer reachable.

In table.c, the original implementation
checks:

```c
if (entry->key != NULL &&
    !entry->key->obj.isMarked) {
  tableDelete(table, entry->key);
}
```

With the new marking convention,
I would replace this with:

```c
if (entry->key != NULL &&
    entry->key->obj.mark != vm.markValue) {
  tableDelete(table, entry->key);
}
```

The updated helper becomes:

```c
void tableRemoveWhite(Table* table) {
  for (int i = 0; i < table->capacity; i++) {
    Entry* entry = &table->entries[i];

    if (entry->key != NULL &&
        entry->key->obj.mark != vm.markValue) {
      tableDelete(table, entry->key);
    }
  }
}
```

This ensures that the string table
continues removing unreachable strings
even when the meaning of the mark
bit changes between collections.

This step is important because
the string table must not keep
dangling pointers to freed objects.

## 8. Update the Sweep Phase

This is the main optimization.

In the original sweep() function,
every surviving object has its
isMarked field reset to false.

With mark-bit flipping, that
individual reset is unnecessary.

In memory.c, the updated sweep()
function becomes:

```c
static void sweep() {
  Obj* previous = NULL;
  Obj* object = vm.objects;

  while (object != NULL) {
    // Keep objects marked in this cycle.
    if (object->mark == vm.markValue) {
      previous = object;
      object = object->next;
    } else {
      // Remove an unreachable object.
      Obj* unreached = object;
      object = object->next;

      if (previous != NULL) {
        previous->next = object;
      } else {
        vm.objects = object;
      }

      freeObject(unreached);
    }
  }
}
```

Notice that the following assignment
is no longer present:

```c
object->isMarked = false;
```

Instead, the collector only checks
whether an object's mark matches
the VM's current marking value.

If the values match, the object
was reached during marking and
must be preserved.

If they do not match, the object
is unreachable and can be freed.

The sweep phase continues to
traverse the object list, but
it no longer clears the marking
field on each surviving object.

## 9. Flip the Marking Value After Collection

After the mark, trace, and sweep
phases have finished, the VM can
change which Boolean value
represents a marked object.

In memory.c, add the following
line near the end of collectGarbage():

```c
vm.markValue = !vm.markValue;
```

The relevant sequence becomes:

```c
void collectGarbage() {
#ifdef DEBUG_LOG_GC
  printf("-- gc begin\n");
  size_t before = vm.bytesAllocated;
#endif

  markRoots();
  traceReferences();
  tableRemoveWhite(&vm.strings);
  sweep();

  vm.nextGC =
      vm.bytesAllocated * GC_HEAP_GROW_FACTOR;

  // Prepare the next collection cycle.
  vm.markValue = !vm.markValue;

#ifdef DEBUG_LOG_GC
  printf("-- gc end\n");
  printf(
      "   collected %zu bytes (from %zu to %zu)"
      " next at %zu\n",
      before - vm.bytesAllocated,
      before,
      vm.bytesAllocated,
      vm.nextGC);
#endif
}
```

The timing of the flip is important.

It must happen after sweeping because
the current collection uses vm.markValue
to determine which objects survived.

If the meaning were reversed before
sweeping finished, live objects
could be incorrectly treated
as unreachable.

## 10. Why This Works

Suppose the VM starts with:

```text
vm.markValue = true
```

During the first collection, the
collector marks reachable objects
by setting their mark field to true.

After sweeping, every surviving
object has:

```text
object->mark = true
```

At the end of the collection,
the VM flips the meaning:

```text
vm.markValue = false
```

Now those same surviving objects
are considered unmarked because:

```text
object->mark != vm.markValue
```

During the next collection, the
collector marks reachable objects
by setting their mark field to false.

After that collection, the VM
flips the marking value back
to true.

This repeats for every cycle.

The collector never needs to
reset the mark field on every
surviving object individually.

Instead, one Boolean update
changes the meaning of every
object's existing marking bit.

## 11. Example of Two Collection Cycles

The process can be summarized
using this example:

```text
Collection 1:
vm.markValue = true

Object A is reachable:
A.mark = true

Object B is unreachable:
B.mark = false

Sweep:
Keep A
Free B

End:
vm.markValue = false
```

At the beginning of the next cycle:

```text
Collection 2:
vm.markValue = false

Object A begins logically unmarked:
A.mark = true

Mark A again:
A.mark = false

Sweep:
Keep A

End:
vm.markValue = true
```

The same object survives both
collections without requiring
a separate reset after each sweep.

## 12. Testing the Optimization

To test the implementation,
I would enable the garbage
collector's debug flags.

In common.h:

```c
#define DEBUG_STRESS_GC
#define DEBUG_LOG_GC
```

DEBUG_STRESS_GC forces frequent
garbage collection during allocations.

DEBUG_LOG_GC prints information
about marking, allocation,
and garbage collection.

This would help identify cases
where objects are freed too early
or accidentally retained.

I would also run programs
that create many temporary strings
and closures.

For example:

```lox
fun makeClosure(value) {
  fun inner() {
    return value;
  }

  return inner;
}

var saved = makeClosure("still alive");

var i = 0;
while (i < 1000) {
  var temporary = "hello" + " world";
  i = i + 1;
}

print saved();
```

Expected output:

```text
still alive
```

The string and closure captured
by saved should remain reachable
despite repeated allocations
and garbage collection cycles.

The test is intended to verify
correct object reachability.

It does not provide a
performance measurement.

## 13. Performance Considerations

The main potential benefit of
mark-bit flipping is that the
collector no longer needs to
write to the marking field
of every surviving object
during sweeping.

Instead, it updates one
Boolean field in the VM
after each collection.

This could reduce memory writes
during the sweep phase,
especially when many objects
survive a collection.

However, the collector must
compare each object's marking
value against the VM's
current marking value.

This comparison is required
during marking, weak-reference
cleanup, and sweeping.

Therefore, the optimization
changes the work performed
by the collector rather than
eliminating all marking overhead.

I have not run performance
benchmarks, so I cannot
determine whether it
improves collection speed
or overall execution time.

The actual results would depend
on the program's allocation
patterns, the number of
surviving objects, and the
system being used.

## 14. Is the Optimization Worth It?

I think this optimization
is worth exploring because
it avoids repeatedly modifying
the marking field of every
surviving object.

The changes are also fairly
localized to the memory
management system.

The main disadvantage is that
the interpretation of the
mark bit becomes less
straightforward.

Instead of checking whether
isMarked is true, the VM
must compare each object's
mark field with vm.markValue.

If even one part of the
collector continues using
the original Boolean
interpretation, it could
incorrectly mark or free
objects.

Therefore, consistency
is especially important.

Overall, I think this is
a reasonable optimization,
but I would want to
benchmark it before
claiming any performance
benefit.

## 15. Compatibility With Challenge 1

Challenge 1 proposes replacing
the Obj structure with a packed
64-bit header.

Challenge 2 instead assumes
that the Obj structure has
a separate Boolean mark field.

If both challenges are
implemented together,
the two designs must be
combined carefully.

Rather than directly accessing
object->mark, the collector
would need to use the
packed-header accessor
and setter functions.

For example, the logic
would conceptually become:

```c
if (isMarked(object) == vm.markValue) {
  // Object is marked in the current cycle.
}
```

And marking would use:

```c
setIsMarked(object, vm.markValue);
```

New objects would begin with:

```c
setIsMarked(object, !vm.markValue);
```

The vm.markValue field
would still be flipped
after every collection.

These are conceptual integration
changes. The packed-header
implementation also requires
its own pointer-representation
safety checks.
